WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.360 --> 00:00:07.240
There are good reasons you see
so many people with Latin tattoos.

00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:11.320
For a start, Latin is a highly
economical language -

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:13.760
you can say a lot
in just a handful of words.

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:15.920
Another reason
is that Latin literature

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:18.040
is full of timeless truisms.

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:21.000
Observations made
over 2,000 years ago

00:00:21.080 --> 00:00:24.440
are often as applicable to us now
as they were to the Romans.

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:26.920
Here are five quotes
from Latin authors

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:29.480
I feel are just as relevant
to us today.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:31.000
Number one:

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:35.960
Non videmus manticae
quod in tergo est.

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:39.200
We cannot see the baggage
that's on our own back.

00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:41.120
In the mid-1st Century BC,

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:45.000
the Latin love poet, Catullus,
observed a common human trait.

00:00:45.080 --> 00:00:48.640
While we find it easy to
recognise faults in other people,

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:50.680
we tend to be more or less
blind to our own.

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:54.400
"Each of us has been assigned
his own fault," he wrote,

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:57.000
"but we cannot see the baggage
that's on our own back."

00:00:57.400 --> 00:01:00.840
Catullus' message then,
from over 2,000 years ago,

00:01:00.920 --> 00:01:04.120
is to think about your own faults
before criticising others.

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:06.040
Two:

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:07.960
Omnia vincit amor.

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:10.480
Love conquers all.

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:14.680
Love, in Virgil's line,
isn't just a human emotion,

00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:16.320
it's a divine force.

00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:19.800
Love, amor, is Cupid,

00:01:19.880 --> 00:01:22.000
the son of Venus,
the goddess of love.

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:26.160
He will strike you with his arrow
and make you fall hopelessly in love.

00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:29.520
The love-sick poet who sighed,
"Omnia vincit amor"

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:32.200
knew that to be in love isn't
necessarily to be happy.

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:34.160
To be in love is also to be in pain.

00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:36.840
Virgil shows us there's no point
in trying to fight it.

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:41.120
"Omnia vincit amor,
et nos cedamus amori" he continues.

00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:43.160
"You're better off yielding to love

00:01:43.240 --> 00:01:45.520
than trying to shield yourself
from his spell."

00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:47.720
Three:

00:01:47.800 --> 00:01:52.280
Si hortum in bibliotheca
habes deerit nihil.

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:56.720
If you have a garden and a library,
you'll lack nothing.

00:01:58.000 --> 00:01:59.640
Cicero actually wrote,

00:01:59.720 --> 00:02:02.960
"If you have a garden
in the library, you'll lack nothing."

00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:06.520
Houses in Rome typically had the
garden in the middle of the house

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:07.920
rather than at the back.

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:11.680
In the ancient world there was
a tradition for learning outside.

00:02:12.040 --> 00:02:14.720
Students at Plato's academy
in Greece, for instance,

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:16.400
used to learn among the trees.

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:20.520
Cicero's message is if you have books
and nature you have everything.

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:21.920
Four:

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:24.320
In vino veritas.

00:02:25.160 --> 00:02:26.520
In wine is truth.

00:02:27.240 --> 00:02:30.080
Anyone who has drunk
a few glasses can vouch for this.

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:31.640
Wine loosens the tongue.

00:02:31.720 --> 00:02:34.920
It may make you talk a lot
of nonsense but as the ancients knew,

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:37.160
it could also make you
spill your secrets.

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:41.080
The historian Pliny the Elder
alluded to a popular saying,

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:43.360
about wine bringing out the truth.

00:02:43.640 --> 00:02:45.120
"Veritas vino est."

00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:49.000
At the same time, he pitied the
drunks who do not see the Sun rising

00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:51.280
and therefore live shorter lives.

00:02:51.640 --> 00:02:52.640
Five:

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:58.200
Donec eris sospes
multos numerabis amicos.

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:02.240
For as long as you're doing well,
you'll have many friends.

00:03:02.720 --> 00:03:05.640
Ovid was one of the leading poets
of his day

00:03:05.720 --> 00:03:07.080
but fell foul of the emperor

00:03:07.160 --> 00:03:09.760
and was banished to the coast
of modern Romania.

00:03:09.880 --> 00:03:12.400
While there he wrote
about his miserable fate,

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:14.960
which he blamed on a poem
and a mistake.

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:18.320
The poem was full of advice
about how to love and make love

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:20.880
and was far too
raunchy for his times.

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:24.880
What the mistake was remains
a mystery to this day.

00:03:25.080 --> 00:03:28.840
In exile, the once popular poet
found himself friendless.

00:03:29.080 --> 00:03:31.880
The message is surely to work out
who your real friends are

00:03:31.960 --> 00:03:33.080
before it's too late.

